msgid "Open, enter, create"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*The file, the room and the published study: where the copy you are "
"editing lives, and the three verbs that reach it.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Before starting to work, one question decides everything else: **where "
"does the copy you are editing live?** It has two answers. Either it lives"
" on your disk, and it is a file of yours; or it lives on a StratiGraph "
"node, and then it is a room. EMStudio names the gesture that reaches each"
" place with a verb."
msgstr ""

msgid "The three verbs"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Open** a file from the disk."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``File ▸ Open…`` reads an ``em.json`` of yours. Your computer is all it "
"needs: you are the keeper of the file, and saving writes it back where it"
" came from. This is how EMStudio works on its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Enter** a room."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A room is a study that lives on a node. Entering it attaches you to the "
"room where the study already is: you receive its graph, and from then on "
"what you write is written there, for every member, under your name. The "
"rooms of a node are listed in the chip's menu, under **My space**."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Create** a room."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``File ▸ Create a room…`` makes a new, empty room on your node; ``File ▸ "
"Bring this into a new room…`` makes a room from the study you have open, "
"with its files. Whoever creates a room is its owner: ownership is born "
"from the gesture."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A study that lives on the node is always a room, even when you are its "
"only member. A room with one member is your own table on the node, and it"
" can welcome the others later. Working online therefore always means "
"working in a room. The web version of EMStudio can also open an "
"``em.json`` from your computer and give it back as a download, which is "
"the verb *open* carried into the browser."
msgstr ""

msgid "Three keepers, three words"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The file is an object, the room is a place, the published study is a "
"citation.**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **file** on your disk is yours. You open it, change it, save it, copy "
"it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **room** is a place where a study is kept and worked on by its members,"
" each with a role: a *viewer* reads, an *editor* writes, an *admin* also "
"manages the people, the *owner* created it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **published study** is a version that a node's Catalog has fixed and "
"that others can cite. EMStudio opens it from the Catalog as a copy to "
"work on; the published version stays exactly as it was cited. New work on"
" it continues in a file or in a room, and may become a new published "
"version in its turn."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One passes from one keeper to another only with an explicit gesture: "
"**Bring this into a new room…** turns a file into a room; ``File ▸ Save "
"As…``, inside a room, makes a copy of the room's study on your disk; a "
"node publishes a room's study in its Catalog. Each gesture is named, so "
"that there are never two copies that both believe they are the original."
msgstr ""

msgid "One room, one living graph"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A room is the living copy of its study. Two groups who work on the same "
"study work in the same room, as members; a project that divides its work "
"divides it by graphs inside the same study, not by parallel rooms. Two "
"rooms can share files: the node keeps each file once, under the "
"fingerprint of its content, and both rooms cite it."
msgstr ""

msgid "What travels, and how far"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Three kinds of thing travel between the people of a room and between your"
" own programs, and each has its own reach."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The study** (the graph, the documents, the provenance of the files) "
"reaches the whole room. A unit you add is there for every member, with "
"your name on it. When two people change the same field, the room keeps "
"one value and EMStudio says whose change was kept."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Presence** reaches the room as information. You see who is in the room "
"and which nodes each one has selected, marked on your canvas. Another "
"person's selection never moves yours, and yours never moves theirs."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The live link between your own programs** reaches only you. With the "
"mode **With an app**, a unit selected in EMStudio is selected in Blender "
"and the other way round (see :doc:`/how-to/extensions/live-sync-with-em-"
"tools`). This tight link belongs to one person's tools, on the same "
"computer or the same network; it can stay on while you are in a room."
msgstr ""

msgid "Where you work"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The chip at the top right of the window carries the place you are working"
" in, in its section **Where you work**. Under **Mode** it offers three "
"choices:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**On this computer**: the files of your disk, the verb *open*;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**With an app**: the live link with EM Tools in Blender;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**In a room**: the last room you were in on your node, the verb *enter*."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The status bar repeats the place: **Room** followed by the name of the "
"room and of the node, when you are in one. Earlier versions of EMStudio "
"called these ways of working *Standalone*, *Sidecar* and *Hub*. The three"
" choices press buttons that still carry the older names, **Standalone**, "
"**Sidecar (sync)** and **Join Room**, as :doc:`/reference/menus` lists "
"them."
msgstr ""

